    CEC bench answer file - Windows 11 Pro, x64.

    GOAL: zero technician clicks from "boot the installer" to "the provisioning
    pipeline is running on screen". The tech picks the target disk (deliberately
    NOT automated - see DiskConfiguration below), and everything after that is
    hands-off through OOBE, auto-logon, and the CEC-Autosetup pipeline starting
    itself.

    USAGE: place this file named exactly  autounattend.xml  at the ROOT of the
    Windows install USB. Put the tool alongside it as  \CEC-Autosetup\  (the
    launcher below finds it on any drive letter).

    ============================ EDIT BEFORE USE ============================
    Every line tagged CHANGEME must be reviewed for your shop. At minimum:
      * bench account name + password (oobeSystem / UserAccounts + AutoLogon)
      * TimeZone
      * ComputerName scheme
    ==========================================================================

    SECURITY NOTE: the bench account password lives in PLAINTEXT (or trivially
    reversible base64) in this file, on a USB stick that walks around the shop.
    Treat it as a throwaway local bench credential, never a password reused
    anywhere else, and consider having the ship-out stage remove/rename the
    account. See unattend/README.md.

    VERIFICATION STATUS: structure and element names below follow the standard
    Windows unattend schema, but Microsoft has changed OOBE/local-account
    behavior across recent Windows 11 builds. Confirm against a scratch VM on
    the exact image you deploy before trusting it on a customer build. Items
    flagged VERIFY are the ones most likely to have shifted.
-->
<unattend xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:unattend">

    <!-- =====================================================================
         PASS 1: windowsPE - the installer environment itself.
         Kills the language/keyboard prompt and the EULA click.
         ===================================================================== -->
    <settings pass="windowsPE">

        <component name="Microsoft-Windows-International-Core-WinPE"
                   processorArchitecture="amd64"
                   publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35"
                   language="neutral"
                   versionScope="nonSxS">
            <SetupUILanguage>
                <UILanguage>en-US</UILanguage>
            </SetupUILanguage>
            <InputLocale>0409:00000409</InputLocale>   <!-- CHANGEME if not US keyboard -->
            <SystemLocale>en-US</SystemLocale>
            <UILanguage>en-US</UILanguage>
            <UserLocale>en-US</UserLocale>
        </component>

        <component name="Microsoft-Windows-Setup"
                   processorArchitecture="amd64"
                   publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35"
                   language="neutral"
                   versionScope="nonSxS">

            <UserData>
                <!-- REQUIRED for any unattended install. Different element from
                     HideEULAPage below; without this, Setup shows UI. -->
                <AcceptEula>true</AcceptEula>
                <!--
                    EDITION FORCING - keep this even though the image is pinned
                    below. On a board whose firmware carries an embedded HOME
                    key (MSDM), Setup will silently auto-select Home and skip
                    edition selection entirely - you get a Home machine and no
                    prompt telling you so. This generic Pro key overrides that.

                    It is Microsoft's published generic Pro key: it selects the
                    edition and does NOT activate. Activation still comes from
                    the firmware key or your licensing.
                    (An INVALID key here makes Setup fail outright - don't
                    substitute a random key.)
                -->
                <ProductKey>
                    <Key>VK7JG-NPHTM-C97JM-9MPGT-3V66T</Key>
                    <WillShowUI>Never</WillShowUI>
                </ProductKey>
            </UserData>

            <!--
                NOTE: there is deliberately NO <ImageInstall> block.

                The ProductKey above already selects the edition, so an
                <InstallFrom>/<MetaData> pin would be a redundant second
                mechanism - and there are field reports of <InstallFrom> itself
                causing failures on 24H2 when it isn't genuinely needed. Fewer
                moving parts is the safer trade.

                Critically, omitting <ImageInstall> also means there is no
                <InstallTo>, which is half of what makes the disk picker appear.
                (<InstallTo> hardcodes DiskID/PartitionID independently of
                <DiskConfiguration> - leaving one in while removing the other
                still silently targets a fixed disk.) Never use
                <InstallToAvailablePartition>: it searches from disk 0 through
                every disk and will happily land on a customer's data drive, and
                setting it together with <InstallTo> fails the install outright.
            -->

            <!--
                ============ DISK CONFIGURATION: DELIBERATELY OMITTED ============
                Omitting <DiskConfiguration> AND <ImageInstall> is what surfaces
                the "Where do you want to install Windows?" picker. Everything
                else in this file stays automated. This is a considered decision,
                not an unfinished section.

                WHY - DiskID is a bare ordinal with no stability guarantee.
                Microsoft's entire definition is "0 specifies the first disk"; it
                never defines "first", and never warns that the mapping is
                firmware-dependent. In practice enumeration follows controller
                order, and on plenty of desktop boards the NVMe OS drive is NOT
                disk 0 - a documented firsthand case on an ASUS Prime Z590-A had
                HDDs enumerate first, putting the OS drive at disk 2.

                THE TRAP THAT SHOULD DECIDE IT FOR US: if the internal NVMe is
                invisible to WinPE, the USB stick becomes disk 0 and a
                wipe-disk-0 answer file cleans the installer media. The most
                common cause of that invisibility is Intel RST/VMD enabled in
                BIOS without the driver loaded into WinPE - i.e. exactly the VMD
                configuration the driver pipeline already has to reason about.
                With manual selection that same situation shows up harmlessly as
                "no drives found" instead of a wiped stick.

                Manual selection also means ONE answer file works on every
                machine regardless of drive layout, with no per-unit editing -
                and it leaves a human eyeball on the one irreversible step. It is
                about four seconds per build.

                IF YOU EVER AUTOMATE IT, the non-obvious rules:
                  * Use native <DiskConfiguration>/<WillWipeDisk>, NOT a diskpart
                    'clean' script. WillWipeDisk is documented to refuse the disk
                    running Setup, the booted OS disk, and one holding an active
                    page file. diskpart clean has NO such guard - which is why
                    every firsthand wiped-USB-stick report comes from diskpart
                    tooling.
                  * Add pre-flight assertions (capacity range, bus type, "no
                    existing partitions") that HALT setup on mismatch.
                  * Recovery partition: 990 MB minimum per Microsoft's current
                    guidance, placed IMMEDIATELY AFTER the Windows partition so
                    WinRE updates can grow it. Microsoft's own sample scripts
                    still say 500 MB - that is stale and manufactures the
                    KB5034441 / 0x80070643 failure into every machine you ship.
                  * Never assign drive letter X: to a partition - it collides
                    with the WinPE ramdisk and hangs the install on 24H2+.
                  * Do not set <WillShowUI>Never</WillShowUI> with no <Disk>
                    element: documented hard failure.
                  * Confirm the index on the actual hardware first: Shift+F10 ->
                    diskpart -> list disk.
                ================================================================
            -->
        </component>
    </settings>

    <!-- =====================================================================
         PASS 2: specialize - runs after image apply, before OOBE.
         Machine identity + the UAC change, so UAC is already off by the time
         the bench account logs on (EnableLUA takes effect on reboot, and a
         reboot happens between this pass and first logon).
         ===================================================================== -->
    <settings pass="specialize">

        <component name="Microsoft-Windows-Shell-Setup"
                   processorArchitecture="amd64"
                   publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35"
                   language="neutral"
                   versionScope="nonSxS">
            <!-- '*' = random name. CHANGEME to your order-number scheme if you
                 want the name to carry the work order (the pipeline reads the
                 machine name into its reports). -->
            <ComputerName>*</ComputerName>
            <TimeZone>Central Standard Time</TimeZone>   <!-- CHANGEME -->
        </component>

        <component name="Microsoft-Windows-Deployment"
                   processorArchitecture="amd64"
                   publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35"
                   language="neutral"
                   versionScope="nonSxS">
            <RunSynchronous>
                <!--
                    UAC OFF for the provisioning window. specialize is the right
                    pass: it runs as SYSTEM before OOBE, and EnableLUA is read at
                    logon/boot - so it is already in effect by first logon. (Doing
                    this in FirstLogonCommands would be too late to help the very
                    commands being run.)

                    The pipeline's WindowsPrep stage detects it is already 0 and
                    records that; the ship-out stage restores it before delivery.
                    If you delete these, the runtime stage sets them instead - same
                    end state, one extra reboot to take effect.

                    Worth knowing: FirstLogonCommands under an ADMIN account already
                    run elevated, so the pipeline itself does not strictly require
                    UAC-off. This is belt-and-braces against third-party installers
                    that would otherwise raise a consent dialog mid-run.
                    Tradeoff: EnableLUA=0 is known to break some UWP/Store apps and
                    Settings pages - which is fine for a bench window, and exactly
                    why it must be restored before ship.
                -->
                <RunSynchronousCommand wcm:action="add" xmlns:wcm="http://schemas.microsoft.com/WMIConfig/2002/State">
                    <Order>1</Order>
                    <Description>Disable UAC for the provisioning window</Description>
                    <Path>reg add "HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Policies\System" /v EnableLUA /t REG_DWORD /d 0 /f</Path>
                </RunSynchronousCommand>
                <RunSynchronousCommand wcm:action="add" xmlns:wcm="http://schemas.microsoft.com/WMIConfig/2002/State">
                    <Order>2</Order>
                    <Description>No elevation prompt for administrators</Description>
                    <Path>reg add "HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Policies\System" /v ConsentPromptBehaviorAdmin /t REG_DWORD /d 0 /f</Path>
                </RunSynchronousCommand>

                <!--
                    ================= NETWORK OFF DURING OOBE =================
                    This is a THROUGHPUT AND RELIABILITY control, not a security
                    one, and it is the single highest-value line in this file.

                    Microsoft documents that critical zero-day-patch (ZDP) updates
                    download automatically during OOBE "after the user has
                    connected to a network" and that "the user can't opt out".
                    There is NO answer-file, policy, or registry setting that
                    disables it - the ONLY documented lever is the trigger
                    condition itself: no network, no ZDP.

                    Why we care, beyond the 30+ minutes it can burn:
                    a mid-OOBE update RESTART leaves the machine NOT automatically
                    signed in, which is documented to break autologon provisioning
                    - i.e. it can strand this very hand-off before the pipeline
                    ever starts.

                    We lose nothing by deferring: the pipeline runs Windows Update
                    to full completion itself (stage 2), on our schedule, with our
                    reboot/resume handling and our logging.

                    Adapters are re-enabled by the FirstLogonCommands launcher
                    below, immediately before the pipeline starts.

                    TO DISABLE THIS BEHAVIOR: delete this command AND the
                    Enable-NetAdapter call in FirstLogonCommands, then simply
                    leave the ethernet unplugged during install instead (same
                    effect, manual). If the re-enable ever fails, the machine
                    comes up offline - the pipeline reports that clearly in its
                    environment snapshot and degrades to its offline path rather
                    than failing, and Network Connections fixes it by hand.
                -->
                <RunSynchronousCommand wcm:action="add" xmlns:wcm="http://schemas.microsoft.com/WMIConfig/2002/State">
                    <Order>3</Order>
                    <Description>Disable network adapters for OOBE (avoids mandatory ZDP updates + the reboot that breaks autologon)</Description>
                    <Path>powershell.exe -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -Command "Get-NetAdapter | Disable-NetAdapter -Confirm:$false -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue"</Path>
                </RunSynchronousCommand>

                <!--
                    ============ AUTOMATIC DEVICE ENCRYPTION CONTROL ============
                    READ unattend/README.md BEFORE CHANGING THIS. It is a shop
                    POLICY decision, not a purely technical one, and there is a
                    licensing angle.

                    What actually happens on 24H2/25H2: Microsoft dropped the old
                    Modern Standby/HSTI prerequisites, so a clean install on a
                    machine with TPM + Secure Boot + PCR7 support encrypts the
                    drive during OOBE regardless of account type.

                    On a LOCAL-ACCOUNT machine it stops half way: the volume ends
                    up encrypted with a CLEAR KEY - the equivalent of BitLocker
                    "suspended". Verified state on a real 25H2 Pro local-account
                    box: 100% encrypted, "Protection Off", Control Panel says
                    "waiting for activation".

                    That means it is NOT a lockout risk (the TPM is never
                    consulted, so BIOS flashes and CMOS clears cannot trigger a
                    recovery prompt) - but it is ALSO NOT SECURITY. The key sits
                    unprotected on the disk; someone demonstrated reading such a
                    drive by booting a WinPE stick. Shipping that state gives the
                    customer the performance cost of encryption, a Control Panel
                    that says "encrypted", and zero actual protection.

                    So the real choice is: ship DECRYPTED (this setting), or ship
                    genuinely ARMED with the recovery key printed and boxed.
                    Shipping the half-state is the one option to avoid.

                    LICENSING CAVEAT on this setting: Microsoft's OEM BitLocker
                    doc says disabling device encryption outside of "OEM
                    implements their own encryption technology" is "prohibited as
                    it violates the Windows 11 licensing requirements". Weigh
                    that before standardising on it.

                    Either way the ship-out stage MUST verify rather than assume:
                    'manage-bde -status' (0.0% vs 100% is the discriminator -
                    both decrypted and clear-key states report "Protection Off")
                    and 'manage-bde -protectors -get C:' to catch a recovery
                    password that exists but was never shown to anyone.

                    NOTE: many DIY desktops never auto-encrypt at all - a discrete
                    GPU or add-in card with an option ROM breaks PCR7 binding.
                    Behaviour varies per build, so test per configuration.
                -->
                <RunSynchronousCommand wcm:action="add" xmlns:wcm="http://schemas.microsoft.com/WMIConfig/2002/State">
                    <Order>4</Order>
                    <Description>Prevent automatic device encryption during OOBE (see README - policy + licensing decision)</Description>
                    <Path>reg add "HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\BitLocker" /v PreventDeviceEncryption /t REG_DWORD /d 1 /f</Path>
                </RunSynchronousCommand>
            </RunSynchronous>
        </component>
    </settings>

    <!-- =====================================================================
         PASS 3: oobeSystem - the out-of-box experience.
         This is where the click-elimination happens.
         ===================================================================== -->
    <settings pass="oobeSystem">

        <component name="Microsoft-Windows-International-Core"
                   processorArchitecture="amd64"
                   publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35"
                   language="neutral"
                   versionScope="nonSxS">
            <InputLocale>0409:00000409</InputLocale>   <!-- CHANGEME with the WinPE one -->
            <SystemLocale>en-US</SystemLocale>
            <UILanguage>en-US</UILanguage>
            <UserLocale>en-US</UserLocale>
        </component>

        <component name="Microsoft-Windows-Shell-Setup"
                   processorArchitecture="amd64"
                   publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35"
                   language="neutral"
                   versionScope="nonSxS">

            <OOBE>
                <!--
                    HideEULAPage: LICENSING CAVEAT, not a technical one.
                    Microsoft's OEM/System Builder terms say this may be used
                    "only for testing prior to shipment" - System Builder
                    licensing expects the end user to get the chance to accept
                    the EULA. As a system builder, that's your call to make;
                    set it false if you want the customer to see the EULA on
                    the machine you deliver.
                -->
                <HideEULAPage>true</HideEULAPage>   <!-- CHANGEME: see licensing note -->
                <HideOEMRegistrationScreen>true</HideOEMRegistrationScreen>
                <!-- Essential for the no-network-during-OOBE flow above. -->
                <HideWirelessSetupInOOBE>true</HideWirelessSetupInOOBE>
                <!--
                    Suppresses the online sign-in page. NOTE: this alone is NOT
                    what bypasses the Microsoft-account wall - the LocalAccount
                    defined below is the load-bearing part. Microsoft has been
                    removing the *interactive* bypasses (BypassNRO.cmd,
                    ms-cxh:localonly) while leaving answer-file account creation
                    as the supported route - but they have never guaranteed it
                    indefinitely, so re-test on each feature update.
                -->
                <HideOnlineAccountScreens>true</HideOnlineAccountScreens>
                <!--
                    NOTE: HideLocalAccountScreen is deliberately NOT set here.
                    Microsoft documents it as applying "only to the Windows
                    Server editions" - on Win11 Pro it is a no-op, despite
                    appearing in most community guides as an MSA-bypass step.
                    Pre-creating the LocalAccount below is what actually stops
                    the "Who's going to use this device?" screen.

                    Also deliberately absent: SkipMachineOOBE / SkipUserOOBE.
                    Both are deprecated, are no longer even listed as valid OOBE
                    child elements, and Microsoft warns they can leave a machine
                    half-configured. Any guide recommending them is stale.
                -->
                <ProtectYourPC>3</ProtectYourPC>   <!-- 3 = skip express settings. NOTE: does NOT control updates on Win11, despite stale Win7-era docs saying so. -->
                <NetworkLocation>Work</NetworkLocation>
            </OOBE>

            <!--
                A LOCAL account is what actually bypasses the "sign in with a
                Microsoft account" wall. CHANGEME: name + password.
                The password is stored here in the clear - throwaway bench
                credential only. See README security note.
            -->
            <UserAccounts>
                <LocalAccounts>
                    <LocalAccount wcm:action="add" xmlns:wcm="http://schemas.microsoft.com/WMIConfig/2002/State">
                        <Name>CECBench</Name>                  <!-- CHANGEME -->
                        <DisplayName>CEC Bench</DisplayName>
                        <Group>Administrators</Group>
                        <Password>
                            <Value>ChangeMe-BenchOnly</Value>  <!-- CHANGEME -->
                            <PlainText>true</PlainText>
                        </Password>
                    </LocalAccount>
                </LocalAccounts>
            </UserAccounts>

            <!--
                Auto-logon so the pipeline starts with no keyboard touch.
                LogonCount governs how many boots auto-login before Windows
                reverts to the normal logon screen.

                IMPORTANT: the pipeline REBOOTS several times (BIOS hand-off,
                Windows Update cycles, driver-order restart boundaries). Each
                consumes one auto-logon. 12 is deliberately generous so a
                multi-reboot WU catch-up can't strand the run at a logon prompt.
                The pipeline's resume task runs as SYSTEM and does not depend on
                this, but the tech-visible console does.

                DOCUMENTED QUIRK: Windows adds 1 to LogonCount when it is
                greater than 0, so 12 here yields ~13 automatic logons. Exactly
                one auto-logon is impossible via LogonCount alone.

                MUST BE CLEARED BEFORE SHIP: the ship-out stage has to zero
                AutoLogonCount and delete DefaultPassword under
                HKLM\...\Winlogon - the autologon password is stored there in
                CLEARTEXT, readable by any authenticated user. See README.
            -->
            <AutoLogon>
                <Enabled>true</Enabled>
                <LogonCount>12</LogonCount>
                <Username>CECBench</Username>                  <!-- CHANGEME: match above -->
                <Password>
                    <Value>ChangeMe-BenchOnly</Value>          <!-- CHANGEME: match above -->
                    <PlainText>true</PlainText>
                </Password>
            </AutoLogon>

            <!--
                THE HAND-OFF: find CEC-Autosetup on whatever drive letter the
                stick landed on and start the pipeline in its own visible window.

                Why this shape:
                  * FIRST it re-enables the network adapters that the specialize
                    pass disabled to dodge OOBE's mandatory ZDP updates. OOBE is
                    over by now, so the network is safe to bring up.
                  * Drive-letter agnostic - scans every filesystem root for
                    \CEC-Autosetup\bootstrap.ps1, then a flat \bootstrap.ps1.
                  * Start-Process + return: FirstLogonCommands finishes
                    immediately so the desktop appears, while the pipeline runs
                    in its own -NoExit window the tech can watch and scroll.
                    (RequiresUserInput=false also caps the desktop wait at ~2
                    minutes regardless, so a long run never holds it hostage.)
                  * If the stick is missing, it says so loudly instead of
                    failing silently.

                DELIBERATELY ONE COMMAND: Microsoft's own docs contradict each
                other on whether multiple SynchronousCommands run sequentially or
                simultaneously. Keeping everything in a single command - and
                doing any further sequencing inside the pipeline - sidesteps the
                ambiguity entirely.

                DRY-RUN VARIANT: drop the '-Install' argument to make a bare run,
                which performs the readiness SELF-CHECK and installs nothing.
                Recommended for the first machine you test this file on.
            -->
            <FirstLogonCommands>
                <SynchronousCommand wcm:action="add" xmlns:wcm="http://schemas.microsoft.com/WMIConfig/2002/State">
                    <Order>1</Order>
                    <Description>Re-enable networking, then launch the CEC-Autosetup pipeline</Description>
                    <RequiresUserInput>false</RequiresUserInput>
                    <CommandLine>powershell.exe -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -Command "try { Get-NetAdapter | Where-Object { $_.Status -eq 'Disabled' } | Enable-NetAdapter -Confirm:$false -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue } catch { }; $c='CEC-Autosetup\bootstrap.ps1','bootstrap.ps1'; $b=$null; foreach ($d in (Get-PSDrive -PSProvider FileSystem).Root) { foreach ($x in $c) { $p=Join-Path $d $x; if ((Test-Path $p) -and (-not $b)) { $b=$p } } }; if ($b) { Start-Process powershell.exe -ArgumentList @('-NoProfile','-ExecutionPolicy','Bypass','-NoExit','-File',$b,'-Install') } else { Start-Process powershell.exe -ArgumentList @('-NoProfile','-NoExit','-Command','Write-Host ''CEC-Autosetup not found on any drive. Insert the stick and run bootstrap.ps1 -Install manually.'' -ForegroundColor Red') }"</CommandLine>
                </SynchronousCommand>
            </FirstLogonCommands>
        </component>
    </settings>
</unattend>
